<%#
  Empty State — shown in the builder when a workflow has no steps.

  Keeps the real frame rather than a fake one: the list container renders as it
  always does, with a single prompt row inviting the first step.

  Templates lead, and starting blank is the secondary offer. That is the reverse
  of how this read until 2026-09-16, when seven bare step-type buttons dominated
  and templates were a text link beside them. Two things forced the swap:
  a template produces a correctly wired graph with zero errors in one click,
  while adding five steps by hand produced eight errors and four unreachable
  steps; and a first-time editor, watched building unaided, gave up and shipped
  a two-step workflow. Steering people down the path that generates the errors
  was backwards once the other path was actually good.

  The step-type buttons now carry their descriptions rather than hiding them in
  a `title` tooltip — the same descriptions the type picker has always shown.
  The first choice an author makes was the one made blind.

  Local variables:
  - workflow: Workflow object
%>
<div class="builder__empty" id="builder-empty-state">
  <div class="list-section__body">
    <div class="list-row list-row--compact list-row--prompt">
      <span class="list-row__icon" aria-hidden="true">
        <%= icon "plus", class: "icon icon--sm" %>
      </span>
      <div class="list-row__body">
        <div class="list-row__title">Every workflow starts with a step</div>
        <p class="list-row__sub">Add a first step. Then add what happens next from that step.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# Templates reuse the toolbar picker's controller, so "apply" behaves
      identically from both places. This mount has no popover, which is why
      template-picker#close guards on hasPopoverTarget. %>
  <section class="builder__starters"
           data-controller="template-picker"
           data-template-picker-workflow-id-value="<%= workflow.id %>">
    <h2 class="builder__starters-title">Start from a template</h2>
    <p class="builder__starters-sub">Each one is a complete, working example. Open it and change the words.</p>

    <div class="builder__template-cards">
      <% WorkflowTemplate.all.each do |key, template| %>
        <button type="button"
                class="builder__template-card"
                data-action="click->template-picker#apply"
                data-template-key="<%= key %>"
                data-template-name="<%= template["name"] %>"
                style="--template-hue: <%= template["hue"] %>">
          <span class="builder__template-card-head">
            <span class="builder__template-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="builder__template-card-name"><%= template["name"] %></span>
          </span>
          <span class="builder__template-card-desc"><%= template["description"] %></span>
          <span class="builder__template-card-meta"><%= pluralize(template["steps"].size, "step") %></span>
        </button>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="builder__starters builder__starters--secondary">
    <h2 class="builder__starters-title">Or start with a single step</h2>

    <div class="builder__quick-starts">
      <% step_type_options.each do |qs| %>
        <%= button_to workflow_steps_path(workflow),
            method: :post,
            params: { step_type: qs[:type] },
            data: { turbo_stream: true },
            class: "builder__quick-start" do %>
          <span class="builder__quick-start-name"><%= qs[:name] %></span>
          <span class="builder__quick-start-desc"><%= qs[:desc] %></span>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
